+## 8. Elementary probability inequalities used above
+
+For completeness, the two general inequalities used in the lower proof have short derivations. All input distributions here have finite support.
+
+**Bounded differences.** Let F depend on independent coordinates, with changing coordinate i alone changing F by at most c_i. Reveal coordinates in order and let Z_i be the Doob martingale increments. Conditional on the revealed prefix, Z_i has mean zero and lies in an interval of length at most c_i: integrate over the same independent remaining coordinates when comparing two possible values of coordinate i. For any centered random variable Z in an interval of length c, put g(lambda)=log E exp(lambda Z). Its second derivative is the variance under the exponentially tilted law, which is at most c^2/4. Indeed, if the interval is [a,b], then (Z-a)(b-Z)>=0 gives Var(Z)<= (E Z-a)(b-E Z)<= (b-a)^2/4 under any law on that interval. As g(0)=g'(0)=0, integration gives g(lambda)<=lambda^2 c^2/8 for either sign of lambda. Apply this conditional bound to each Z_i and iterate conditional expectations to obtain
+
+    E exp(lambda(F-E F)) <= exp(lambda^2 sum_i c_i^2/8).
+
+Markov's inequality and optimization at lambda=4t/sum_i c_i^2 give Pr(F-E F>=t)<=exp(-2t^2/sum_i c_i^2). Apply the same argument to -F and add the tails. The zero-sensitivity case is deterministic and immediate. This proves the inequality used in section 4 without assuming independent count summands.
+
+**Pinsker for finite laws.** Let P,Q be finite probability laws. If P has positive mass where Q is zero, KL(P||Q) is infinite and the bound is immediate. Otherwise take A={x:P(x)>Q(x)}, so TV(P,Q)=P(A)-Q(A). The log-sum inequality on A and its complement yields KL(P||Q)>=kl(P(A)||Q(A)). To derive log-sum, write each group's sum as its P-mass times the expectation of log(P(x)/Q(x)) under normalized P weights; convexity of -log gives a lower bound equal to that mass times the log of the ratio of group masses. Zero group masses follow by continuity. For fixed q in (0,1), binary divergence f(p)=kl(p||q) has f(q)=f'(q)=0 and f''(p)=1/[p(1-p)]>=4. Integrating twice from q to p gives f(p)>=2(p-q)^2. Boundary cases follow by continuity or infinite divergence. Consequently KL(P||Q)>=2 TV(P,Q)^2, which is precisely the Pinsker estimate used in section 6. KL additivity for independent products follows by expanding the log of their product-density ratio into a sum and taking expectations.
+
